   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "In “An Improvement in Boole’s Calculus of Logic” (1867) Peirce hints at the need for an improvement of Boolean logic, not in the context of predicate logic, but in its inability to express existential statements in the context of term logic. His 1870 paper “Description of a Notation for the Logic of Relatives, Resulting from an Amplification of the Conceptions of Boole’s Calculus” (DNLR) reveals his ambition to marry Boole’s algebraic notation with De Morgan’s effort at relational representation",
   "md_text": "In “An Improvement in Boole’s Calculus of Logic” (1867) Peirce hints at the need for an improvement of Boolean logic, not in the context of predicate logic, but in its inability to express existential statements in the context of term logic. His 1870 paper “Description of a Notation for the Logic of Relatives, Resulting from an Amplification of the Conceptions of Boole’s Calculus” (DNLR) reveals his ambition to marry Boole’s algebraic notation with De Morgan’s effort at relational representation"
  },
  {
   "i": 33,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "For the rest of the third section, four kinds of algebraic signs are introduced to be applied on these predicate letters: the inclusion sign ( ⟦M⟧ ), the addition sign ( ⟦M⟧ ), the multiplication sign (juxtaposition or “,”), and the involution sign (exponentiation).",
   "md_text": "For the rest of the third section, four kinds of algebraic signs are introduced to be applied on these predicate letters: the inclusion sign ( ⟦M⟧ ), the addition sign ( ⟦M⟧ ), the multiplication sign (juxtaposition or “,”), and the involution sign (exponentiation)."
  },
